Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — a few quick points, then a short checklist and next steps.

1) Privacy note
- Don’t share your full SSN in messages or forms unless absolutely required and on a secure site. Last four digits are usually safe to reference; avoid posting the whole number.

2) Short answer
- Whether the expiry is “too close” depends on which country’s visa rules you’re following and the dates of travel. Many countries require your passport to be valid for at least 6 months beyond your planned date of entry or departure; others (including Schengen/Italy) require at least 3 months beyond your planned departure from the area. When in doubt, 6 months’ validity is the safe rule.

3) What I need from you to check precisely
- Your passport expiry date
- Your planned travel dates (entry to and departure from the country)
- Your nationality (passport-issuing country) and which visa/type of entry you’re applying for (tourist/long-stay/etc.)

4) Quick checklist you can use now
- Calculate the time between your passport expiry date and your planned departure (or entry) date.
- If the requirement is “6 months after entry” and your passport has less than 6 months remaining at that reference date, you should renew before applying/ traveling.
- For Schengen (Italy): passport must be valid at least 3 months beyond your planned departure from the Schengen Area and be issued within the last 10 years — but some carriers or embassies may prefer 6 months, so renewing is safest.
- If you need to renew: check your passport agency’s processing times—U.S. routine renewals typically take several weeks; expedited or in-person agency appointments are available for imminent travel.

5) Offer
- If you give me your passport expiry date and your travel dates (and your nationality), I’ll calculate whether you meet a 6-month or 3-month rule and recommend next steps. I can also draft an email to the passport agency or the visa office if you want.

