General guidance you can use right away
- Most countries require your passport to be valid for at least six months beyond your intended date of entry or beyond your planned departure date. Rules vary by country, but the “six-month rule” is common.
- For a six-month visa or a trip lasting up to six months, your passport should typically be valid at least six months after the end of that visa/stay. If your passport expires within six months of your planned departure or the visa’s end date, you should renew before applying or traveling.
- If you’re unsure, check the destination country’s embassy/consulate website or the visa application instructions — that’s the authoritative source. Airlines also check passport validity at check-in, so they enforce the same rules.

Concrete example
- If your trip/visa would end on December 1, 2026, your passport should ideally be valid through at least June 1, 2027. If it expires before that, renew now.

If you want, I can:
- Check the specific entry/visa passport-validity rule for the country you’ll visit (if you tell me which country), or
- Help you calculate whether your passport expiry date is far enough out (tell me the expiry date and your travel dates), or
- Draft an email to the embassy/consulate or to a passport agency if you need to ask about requirements or expedited renewal.

Which would you like me to do?

