General guidance in the meantime:
- For short Schengen visits (tourist/short stay): the common requirement is that your passport be valid for at least 3 months beyond your planned date of departure from the Schengen Area, and normally issued within the last 10 years. Many airlines and some countries prefer — or advise — 6 months’ validity as a safety margin.
- For visa applications or longer stays: embassies/consulates commonly require your passport to be valid for at least 6 months beyond the end date of the visa or intended stay. If you’re applying for a 6‑month visa, you typically need at least 6 months’ validity beyond the visa end date (so in practice the passport should be valid ≈12 months from your application/travel start, depending on the consulate).
- Practical recommendation: if your passport will expire within 6 months of your travel start date or before the end of your intended visa/stay, renew it before applying for the visa or booking travel. That avoids problems at visa processing, at the border, or with airlines.

