Short answer (general rules)
- For travel to Italy/Schengen: the official rule is usually that your passport must be valid for at least 3 months after your planned departure from the Schengen area, and it must have been issued within the last 10 years.  
- However, many airlines and some consulates/relying parties recommend (or effectively require) 6 months’ validity beyond your travel dates to avoid any issues.  
- So if your passport will expire within 6 months of your departure, it’s safer to renew it before traveling.

How to check for your situation (quick steps)
1. Tell me: a) your passport expiry date, and b) your planned departure and return dates. I’ll calculate whether it meets the 3‑month rule and whether it falls within a 6‑month cushion.  
2. Also say which passport country you hold (requirements can vary slightly by nationality).

Example calculation
- If you leave on June 1 and return June 14, add 3 months → passport should be valid through at least Sept 14 (3-month rule). For a 6‑month cushion, it should be valid through Dec 1.

If it’s too close (what to do)
- Renew the passport before travel. Most countries offer expedited processing for an extra fee (timelines vary).  
- If you can’t get the passport renewed before travel, contact the consulate/embassy and your airline to confirm whether they’ll allow travel — but be prepared that you may be denied boarding or entry.  
- If you want, I can draft an email to your passport agency/consulate requesting expedited renewal — tell me which office and any deadlines.

