Useful rules and next steps
- For travel to Italy (Schengen): the formal Schengen rule is that your passport should be valid for at least 3 months beyond your intended date of departure from the Schengen area and issued within the last 10 years. 
- However, many airlines and some countries recommend (or enforce) a 6‑month minimum validity to avoid problems at boarding. So even though Schengen’s formal requirement is 3 months, aim for 6 months to be safe.
- How to check quickly: tell me your passport expiry month/year (just month/year is fine) and your planned return date from Italy (or travel dates). I’ll calculate whether it meets 3‑month and 6‑month thresholds.
- If it doesn’t meet the requirement: renew the passport. Because you’re traveling next month, use expedited renewal options — check your country’s passport agency for emergency/expedited processing or appointment options. Also contact your airline — they can confirm what they’ll accept at check-in.

If you want, tell me:
- your passport expiry month/year, and
- your travel dates (or at least your return date)

I’ll check whether it meets the 3‑month and 6‑month rules and suggest next steps.

