I can help — first, a quick clarification: are you asking about your passport expiry date (not your SSN)? Also tell me your nationality (which passport you hold) and whether you need a Schengen/Italy visa or are traveling visa‑free — that determines the exact rule.

Quick guidance meanwhile:
- For travel to Italy (Schengen) the usual requirement is that your passport be valid for at least 3 months beyond the date you leave the Schengen area, and it must have been issued within the last 10 years. That is the formal Schengen rule.
- Many airlines and some consulates recommend (or effectively require) 6 months’ validity beyond your travel dates — so having less than 6 months remaining can cause problems at check‑in or with visa issuance.
- If you are applying for a visa that lasts six months (or longer), your passport must be valid for the entire visa period and usually for several months beyond it; check the specific consulate instructions.

Practical recommendation:
- If your passport expires within six months of your planned departure, renew it before travel to avoid denial at check‑in or problems with a visa.
- If it expires more than six months after departure but less than three months after return, that could still be risky — aim for at least 6 months’ validity from your travel date if possible.

If you tell me:
- your passport expiry date,
- your nationality (which passport),
- whether you need a visa or not,
I’ll give a definite yes/no and, if needed, draft an email you can send to the consulate or airline asking for confirmation.

