Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — first a quick privacy note: don’t share your full SSN here (you already gave only the last four, which is fine).

A few clarifying questions so I can give a precise answer:
- Do you mean your passport expiry date? (I’ll assume yes unless you tell me otherwise.)
- Which country’s visa are you applying for, and what are your intended travel / visa start and end dates?

General guidance you can use right away
- Many countries (and airlines) recommend — or require — that your passport be valid for at least 6 months beyond your planned date of departure. This is a conservative rule to avoid problems.
- For the Schengen area (including short tourist visits to Italy), the formal rule for short stays (up to 90 days) is typically: passport valid for at least 3 months beyond your intended date of exit from the Schengen zone, and passport issued within the last 10 years. However, some consulates or airlines may still expect 6 months’ validity — so renewing earlier is safer.
- For longer visas (long-stay, work, student, etc.) the requirements vary and may require longer validity.

What I recommend
- If your passport expires less than 6 months after your planned travel dates, renew it before applying — that avoids delays or denial.
- If it expires more than 3 months but less than 6 months after travel and you’re doing a short Schengen visit, it may still be accepted, but check the specific consulate or airline policy to be certain.
- If you’d like, give me the passport expiry date and your travel/visa dates (and the country). I can tell you whether it’s likely okay and can look up the consulate requirement for you.

If you want, I can also:
- Draft an email to the consulate asking about passport validity requirements; or
- Search the official consulate/embassy guidance for the exact rule if you tell me which country and visa type.

